The Sieve of Verona: Prime Numbers, Told Plainly.

Romeo, the number under trial.
Juliet, the divisor who tries him.
Hamlet, the limit of our patience.

                    Act I: Trial by division.

                    Scene I: The limit is read from the audience.

[Enter Hamlet and Romeo]

Romeo:
 Listen to thy heart!

Hamlet:
 Thou art a big cat.

[Exit Hamlet]
[Enter Juliet]

                    Scene II: Every trial begins at two.

Romeo:
 Thou art a big cat.

                    Scene III: Juliet tries every divisor in turn.

Juliet:
 Is the square of myself better than thou?
 If so, let us proceed to scene IV.
 Is the remainder of the quotient between thou and me as bad as nothing?
 If so, let us proceed to scene V.

Romeo:
 Thou art the sum of thyself and a cat.
 Let us return to scene III.

                    Scene IV: A prime is proclaimed.

Juliet:
 Open thy heart!
 Remember thyself.
 Thou art a big big big big big cat.
 Speak thy mind!
 Recall thy former self.

                    Scene V: The next number steps forward.

Juliet:
 Thou art the sum of thyself and a cat.
 Art thou better than Hamlet?
 If so, let us proceed to act II.
 Let us return to scene II.

                    Act II: The end.

                    Scene I: A newline, and a bow.

Juliet:
 Thou art the sum of a big big big cat and a big cat.
 Speak thy mind!

[Exeunt]
